index e5fac9254..36dedfefb 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -350,6 +350,8 @@ Each release's page on GitHub has its notes and measurements. See [the 1.0.0 rel
 
 - In-model runner: an inline `<expr> =` or `A mod M =` that sits inside an open quotation on its line (an odd number of `"` before it, or a `“` with no `”`) is quoted text, and the runner no longer owns its value there (`TF_RUNNER_INLINE_QUOTED`, on by default; 0 restores the earlier trigger). The cell abstains, its abstain record giving the reason `quoted`, and the model's own continuation stands, so nothing is forced and no digit stop is armed. On Qwen3.8-Flash-Next TP1 (MLX 4-bit, one Spark) the model opens its reasoning by quoting the request; when the request itself contained `7^222 =` or `13^150 = <value>`, the runner wrote the 168 to 188 digit value and ` @engine` into the middle of that quotation. In a 9-reply big-power set the first owned span of 7 replies was such a quotation; the model then read the value as the user's text, glued `@engineer…` to the mark and in one reply ran on for 1,024 invented digits. The same expression on the model's own line, or after the quotation closes, is owned as before; of about 12,300 inline spans in 2,506 recorded TP2 replies, 17 sat in an open quotation.
 
+- In-model runner: the reply's runner receipt (`tensorfold.runner` in the response) lists the digit stops that replaced a drawn reasoning token, as `"stops":{"after_value":N,"after_mark":N,"run_cap":N}`, whenever one fired; before, only the serving log recorded them, so a reply whose only runner action was a stop read as one the runner never touched (every cell count 0). A reply with no stop keeps its receipt byte for byte. On Qwen3.8-Flash-Next TP1 a lanes-on opencode reply wrote an invented fib(250) of 1,024 digits in its reasoning; the run cap (`TF_RUNNER_DIGIT_RUN_MAX`) cut it there, the model closed its reasoning and answered without a tool call, and its receipt said nothing of the cut.
+
 - In-model runner: in reasoning, the model's next token right after an owned inline integer may no longer start with a digit, and a reasoning digit run stops at 1,024 digits (`TF_RUNNER_DIGIT_STOP`, `TF_RUNNER_DIGIT_RUN_MAX`; on by default, 0 turns each off). The stopped token becomes a newline where it lands. On Qwen3.8-Flash-Next TP2 the model kept extending owned values such as 7^222 by 1,600-29,700 invented digits, so a reply ran into its thinking budget or `max_tokens` before it answered. Thinking-off replies, fence and REPL results are unchanged. Once a stop fired in a reply, its later reasoning digit runs stop at 64 digits (`TF_RUNNER_DIGIT_HOLD_MAX`; 0 turns it off): after the newline the model restarted a fresh 1,000-digit run each line. With `TF_RUNNER_INLINE_MARK=1` an owned value ends in ` @engine`, and the model's next token may no longer glue a letter, digit, `_`, `-` or `/` to it (`TF_RUNNER_MARK_STOP`, on by default): on Qwen3.8-Flash-Next TP1 the model wrote `@engineer` in 4 of 9 replies of a big-power set, and in one it went on to `@engineer-mike/secret-repo!` and a 1,024-digit run. A space, newline or punctuation after the mark lands as drawn, and a mark stop does not shorten the reply's later digit runs, so the model can still write a number of the same digits, a copy of the value included.
 
 - Server: Responses grows each output item in one buffer, and Kimi chat advances its native stream parser through new tokens, so a long reply retains memory in proportion to its length while every reply and streamed event stays the same (#2642).
diff --git a/zig/src/core/lanes/runner_stop.zig b/zig/src/core/lanes/runner_stop.zig
